1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,556
An engineer wants a "fresh checkout"
before rerunning an evaluation.

2
00:00:08,596 --> 00:00:13,434
They paste a command from a wiki page.
Four seconds later the build artifacts are

3
00:00:13,474 --> 00:00:18,494
gone, as intended. So are a notebook with
a week of error analysis, a .env file with

4
00:00:18,534 --> 00:00:21,686
local settings, and a directory
of model checkpoints.

5
00:00:21,726 --> 00:00:25,179
None of them was tracked: none
had ever been added to Git.

6
00:00:25,519 --> 00:00:30,028
They open the reflog, Git's local
record of where each ref has been.

7
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:34,643
Nothing. They run git fsck, which
lists objects that nothing reaches.

8
00:00:34,683 --> 00:00:38,523
Nothing. They ask you which
command brings the files back.

9
00:00:38,863 --> 00:00:43,521
There's no such command. After this video
you'll be able to say exactly why, and

10
00:00:43,561 --> 00:00:47,514
what they should have typed instead.
Hold on to those two questions.

11
00:00:49,354 --> 00:00:52,915
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:56,811
Two commands today, and they sit
at opposite ends of the undo map.

13
00:00:57,151 --> 00:01:02,160
git clean deletes untracked files. It's the
only undo in Git whose effect nothing in

14
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,121
Git can reverse.

15
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:07,923
git stash does nearly the opposite. It
takes uncommitted work, turns it into

16
00:01:07,963 --> 00:01:12,424
commits, and clears your working tree,
so that the work can come back later.

17
00:01:12,464 --> 00:01:17,103
Because the work becomes objects, almost
everything about stash is recoverable,

18
00:01:17,143 --> 00:01:19,590
including an entry you dropped by mistake.

19
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:24,751
You'll see clean through its dry runs,
which list what would be deleted and delete

20
00:01:24,791 --> 00:01:29,731
nothing. Then stash: what goes into an entry,
apply against pop, the --index option,

21
00:01:29,771 --> 00:01:34,533
a pop that conflicts and the two ways on
from there, and how to recover a dropped

22
00:01:34,573 --> 00:01:39,038
entry. At the end comes a trap with
untracked files that belongs to the lab.

23
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:42,290
After this video you can:

24
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:46,046
Preview and run git clean,
and state why it has no undo.

25
00:01:46,386 --> 00:01:51,183
Park and restore work with stash, including
untracked files and the staged state.

26
00:01:51,523 --> 00:01:56,084
Resolve a conflict from git stash pop,
and explain why the entry was kept.

27
00:01:56,424 --> 00:01:58,899
Recover a dropped stash entry by its ID.

28
00:01:59,239 --> 00:02:02,610
Give three reasons to prefer a
commit on a branch to a stash.

29
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:09,861
Clean. git restore and git reset --hard
leave untracked files alone, with the one

30
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:14,841
exception you saw. The command that
removes them is git clean 🔴 DANGEROUS.

31
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:20,225
It's the only undo whose effect no reflog,
no git fsck and no stash can reverse,

32
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:23,321
because an untracked file
was never an object.

33
00:02:23,661 --> 00:02:28,684
That's the whole reason, and it's the same
question as in the reset video: did Git

34
00:02:28,724 --> 00:02:32,944
ever store it? For an untracked file
the answer is no, by definition.

35
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:39,208
What it changes: the working tree only. It
deletes untracked paths, and ignored ones

36
00:02:39,248 --> 00:02:40,486
too with -x or -X.

37
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:45,893
What it can destroy: anything Git doesn't
track: notebooks, results, .env files,

38
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:47,298
datasets, checkpoints.

39
00:02:47,638 --> 00:02:51,829
How to preview: the same
command with -n in place of -f.

40
00:02:51,869 --> 00:02:56,891
Git insists on one of them: without
-f, -n or -i it refuses to run.

41
00:02:56,931 --> 00:02:59,460
The setting is clean.requireForce.

42
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,524
How to recover: not through Git.

43
00:03:01,864 --> 00:03:06,024
When it's appropriate: after you
have read the dry run line by line.

44
00:03:06,064 --> 00:03:11,088
When in doubt, git stash push -u clears
the same untracked files and keeps them as

45
00:03:11,128 --> 00:03:11,582
objects.

46
00:03:11,922 --> 00:03:16,978
Stash. In one sentence: git stash push 🟡
CAUTION records the index and the working

47
00:03:17,018 --> 00:03:22,135
tree as commits that only refs/stash reaches,
then resets both to HEAD, so that the

48
00:03:22,175 --> 00:03:25,926
work can be brought back later,
on this commit or on another.

49
00:03:26,266 --> 00:03:31,518
Precisely. A stash entry is a commit whose
tree is the state of your tracked files.

50
00:03:31,558 --> 00:03:36,556
Its first parent is the commit that was
HEAD, and its second parent records the

51
00:03:36,596 --> 00:03:40,191
index. With -u, a third parent
holds the untracked files.

52
00:03:40,231 --> 00:03:45,483
refs/stash points at the newest entry, and
older entries live in the reflog of that

53
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:48,162
ref, hence the names stash@{1}, stash@{2}.

54
00:03:48,502 --> 00:03:52,674
git stash apply merges the entry's
changes into the current files.

55
00:03:52,714 --> 00:03:57,716
git stash pop is apply followed by drop,
and the drop happens only if the apply

56
00:03:57,756 --> 00:03:58,354
succeeded.

57
00:03:58,694 --> 00:04:02,933
Inside .git: new commit objects,
the ref refs/stash and its reflog.

58
00:04:02,973 --> 00:04:06,574
HEAD and the branch don't move.
ORIG_HEAD is overwritten.

59
00:04:06,614 --> 00:04:09,512
You saw that cause trouble
in the reset video.

60
00:04:09,852 --> 00:04:13,661
When not to use stash? For anything
that must outlive the day.

61
00:04:13,701 --> 00:04:18,813
The textbook gives three reasons in one
sentence: a commit on a branch shows in git

62
00:04:18,853 --> 00:04:21,979
log, can be pushed, and no
single command drops it.

63
00:04:23,819 --> 00:04:27,913
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy for stash: a labelled tray.

64
00:04:27,953 --> 00:04:33,034
You slide the half-built work off the bench
onto a tray, and the bench is clear for

65
00:04:33,074 --> 00:04:33,960
the urgent job.

66
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:39,367
It breaks in two places. The tray remembers
which commit the work was based on, and

67
00:04:39,407 --> 00:04:43,551
putting it back onto a changed bench
is a merge, which can conflict.

68
00:04:43,591 --> 00:04:48,411
And trays never leave your workshop: a
stash isn't pushed, not fetched, and not

69
00:04:48,451 --> 00:04:49,457
shown by git log.

70
00:04:49,797 --> 00:04:53,279
Now put the two commands side
by side with that picture.

71
00:04:53,319 --> 00:04:58,562
Stash slides things onto a tray. Clean sweeps
the bench into the bin, and the bin is

72
00:04:58,602 --> 00:05:01,895
emptied at once. If you're
not certain, use the tray.

73
00:05:03,735 --> 00:05:07,038
Draw it with me. H is the commit
you're on, with main on it.

74
00:05:07,378 --> 00:05:10,450
I is a child of H. It
records the index as it was.

75
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:15,501
W has two parents, H and I. It records
the tracked files as they were on disk.

76
00:05:15,841 --> 00:05:19,052
W is a commit with two
parents. No branch reaches it.

77
00:05:19,092 --> 00:05:24,204
Only refs/stash does. main hasn't moved.
That's why git log doesn't show your stash,

78
00:05:24,244 --> 00:05:28,988
and why dropping the ref leaves the commit
unreachable but, for a while, still

79
00:05:29,028 --> 00:05:29,478
present.

80
00:05:29,818 --> 00:05:34,514
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper:
redraw the three commits from memory, and

81
00:05:34,554 --> 00:05:37,871
write beside each what it
records. Then say it out loud.

82
00:05:42,711 --> 00:05:46,973
H, the commit you were on. I, the
index. W, your tracked files.

83
00:05:47,013 --> 00:05:50,183
With those three, you can
read any stash entry.

84
00:05:52,023 --> 00:05:56,359
?? is untracked, !! is ignored.
Note .env among the ignored paths.

85
00:05:56,699 --> 00:06:02,491
Bare git clean refuses. Now five dry runs.
For each, predict the list before it

86
00:06:02,531 --> 00:06:05,149
appears. Say it out loud. I'll wait.

87
00:06:17,989 --> 00:06:21,600
Read them as a blast radius
that widens and narrows.

88
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:25,532
Plain -n lists untracked files
in the current directory.

89
00:06:25,572 --> 00:06:31,359
-d adds untracked directories. -X, capital,
lists only ignored paths, which include

90
00:06:31,399 --> 00:06:35,712
.env. -x, lower case, lists both
kinds. -e protects a pattern.

91
00:06:35,752 --> 00:06:41,119
The nested repository vendor/tokenizers
is never on the removal list, and the

92
00:06:41,159 --> 00:06:45,121
tracked edit to serve.yaml
isn't clean's business at all.

93
00:06:45,461 --> 00:06:50,599
The five answers were given a minute ago.
The dry run has been read line by line.

94
00:06:50,639 --> 00:06:51,814
Now the real thing.

95
00:06:57,654 --> 00:07:02,636
The ID that the deleted file's content would
have names no object, and git fsck has

96
00:07:02,676 --> 00:07:07,658
nothing to report. Gone for good. That's
the first answer for the engineer from the

97
00:07:07,698 --> 00:07:10,320
start: why no command
brings the files back.

98
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:17,444
Stash basics. labs/run ch11/stash-basics.
One staged change, one unstaged change,

99
00:07:17,484 --> 00:07:19,044
one untracked file.

100
00:07:19,384 --> 00:07:21,614
Predict git status -s afterwards.

101
00:07:26,454 --> 00:07:31,741
The tracked changes are gone from the files.
The untracked plan.md stayed: a plain

102
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:33,755
stash takes tracked files only.

103
00:07:34,095 --> 00:07:39,222
git stash show summarizes an entry, and
-p prints its full difference from the

104
00:07:39,262 --> 00:07:40,679
commit it was made on.

105
00:07:41,019 --> 00:07:46,461
After apply the entry is still in the list.
And look at rank.py: it was staged when

106
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:51,151
it was stashed and came back unstaged.
Only --index restores the split.

107
00:07:51,491 --> 00:07:56,620
Staged and unstaged, as before. And one
line to remember: "Dropped refs/stash@{0}",

108
00:07:56,660 --> 00:08:01,788
followed by a commit ID in parentheses. pop
dropped the entry and printed the ID of

109
00:08:01,828 --> 00:08:04,840
the commit it let go of.
That line matters later.

110
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:09,770
Here's the whole table: five forms of
push, what goes into the entry, and what's

111
00:08:09,810 --> 00:08:12,954
left behind. The next snippets
walk the last four rows.

112
00:08:13,294 --> 00:08:17,498
With -u the status is clean,
and plan.md is in the entry.

113
00:08:17,838 --> 00:08:19,805
The two that are confused most often:

114
00:08:26,145 --> 00:08:31,285
--staged stashes the index and nothing
else. --keep-index stashes everything and

115
00:08:31,325 --> 00:08:36,594
also leaves the staged part in place, so
you can test exactly what you're about to

116
00:08:36,634 --> 00:08:37,048
commit.

117
00:08:37,388 --> 00:08:40,248
And with a path, only
that path's changes go.

118
00:08:40,588 --> 00:08:44,369
A pop that conflicts.
labs/run ch11/stash-conflict.

119
00:08:44,409 --> 00:08:49,912
Work is stashed, a hotfix changes the
same line, and the stash comes back.

120
00:08:55,252 --> 00:09:00,592
Quick quiz about git stash pop, in two
parts. The exit status: zero or one?

121
00:09:00,632 --> 00:09:04,251
And the entry: A, dropped,
or B, still in the list?

122
00:09:04,291 --> 00:09:05,112
Your answer?

123
00:09:09,952 --> 00:09:14,933
Exit status 1, and B, still in the list.
The last line is the important one: "The

124
00:09:14,973 --> 00:09:20,016
stash entry is kept in case you need it
again." The drop happens only if the apply

125
00:09:20,056 --> 00:09:20,636
succeeded.

126
00:09:20,976 --> 00:09:26,029
It's an ordinary conflict with its own
labels: Updated upstream is the file as it is

127
00:09:26,069 --> 00:09:28,091
now, Stashed changes is the entry.

128
00:09:28,431 --> 00:09:33,544
There are two ways on. One: resolve the
file, clear the conflict state with git

129
00:09:33,584 --> 00:09:37,261
restore --staged or git add,
and drop the entry yourself.

130
00:09:37,301 --> 00:09:42,088
git stash drop 🔴 DANGEROUS: it deletes
the only ref to work that was never

131
00:09:42,128 --> 00:09:45,480
committed. Preview with git
stash show -p stash@{n}.

132
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:49,785
Recovery is by the ID it prints,
for as long as the objects exist.

133
00:09:49,825 --> 00:09:54,938
Appropriate only when the entry's content
is committed elsewhere, or on disk as

134
00:09:54,978 --> 00:09:56,829
here, or you want none of it.

135
00:09:57,169 --> 00:10:02,407
Two: back out with git reset --merge 🔴,
which clears a conflicted index, and use git

136
00:10:02,447 --> 00:10:07,559
stash branch. It creates a branch at the
commit the entry was made on, applies the

137
00:10:07,599 --> 00:10:10,764
entry there, where it can't
conflict, and drops it.

138
00:10:18,104 --> 00:10:23,355
A dropped entry. The objects stay for a
while, and with the ID that drop printed,

139
00:10:23,395 --> 00:10:28,451
c94265d, the entry can be put back. That's
why the line was worth remembering.

140
00:10:28,791 --> 00:10:33,337
git stash store gives the commit
its place in the list again.

141
00:10:33,377 --> 00:10:39,503
Without the ID, the stash manual's git fsck
--unreachable recipe lists candidates.

142
00:10:39,543 --> 00:10:40,705
Lab 8.6 uses it.

143
00:10:41,045 --> 00:10:45,283
The trap, shown last. labs/run
ch11/stash-untracked-trap.

144
00:10:45,323 --> 00:10:51,137
An entry made with -u holds a change to
limits.yaml and an untracked notes.md.

145
00:10:51,177 --> 00:10:55,039
You pop it onto a limits.yaml
that has a local edit.

146
00:10:58,379 --> 00:11:03,575
The pop is refused, the entry is kept, and
look at the last status: notes.md is on

147
00:11:03,615 --> 00:11:08,682
disk. Refused, but not untouched. You move
the local edit out of the way and pop

148
00:11:08,722 --> 00:11:09,065
again.

149
00:11:12,405 --> 00:11:17,050
"notes.md already exists, no checkout."
The tracked change was applied, the

150
00:11:17,090 --> 00:11:22,172
untracked file was refused because it now
exists, and the entry stayed in the list

151
00:11:22,212 --> 00:11:27,169
although all of its content is on disk.
Compare before you drop such an entry by

152
00:11:27,209 --> 00:11:27,481
hand.

153
00:11:27,821 --> 00:11:32,498
The tracked part against the stash
commit: no output means identical.

154
00:11:32,538 --> 00:11:37,831
The untracked part lives in the third
parent of the stash commit, stash@{0}^3.

155
00:11:37,871 --> 00:11:40,156
Identical too. Only then the drop.

156
00:11:41,996 --> 00:11:43,644
Five mistakes to watch for.

157
00:11:43,984 --> 00:11:48,174
Running git clean -f without
the same command with -n first.

158
00:11:48,214 --> 00:11:53,743
Root cause: untracked files were never
objects, so no reflog, fsck or stash can

159
00:11:53,783 --> 00:11:54,871
bring them back.

160
00:11:55,211 --> 00:12:01,444
Using -x where -d was meant. Root cause:
-x adds ignored paths, which is where .env

161
00:12:01,484 --> 00:12:04,769
files, datasets and
checkpoints usually are.

162
00:12:05,109 --> 00:12:08,449
Assuming a plain git stash
push took the new files.

163
00:12:08,489 --> 00:12:12,955
Root cause: without -u an entry
holds changes to tracked files only.

164
00:12:13,295 --> 00:12:17,936
Running git stash drop straight after a
conflicted pop "because pop did not".

165
00:12:17,976 --> 00:12:22,799
Root cause: pop skipped the drop on purpose;
the entry is the only complete copy

166
00:12:22,839 --> 00:12:24,684
until the conflict is resolved.

167
00:12:25,024 --> 00:12:30,223
Keeping a week of work in the stash. Root
cause: an entry is reachable only from

168
00:12:30,263 --> 00:12:35,593
refs/stash, is not pushed, not shown by
git log, and one drop or clear removes its

169
00:12:35,633 --> 00:12:36,182
only ref.

170
00:12:38,022 --> 00:12:43,637
Now, out of the lab. An incident interrupts
a refactoring of per-tenant rate limits

171
00:12:43,677 --> 00:12:49,088
in an inference gateway. The engineer runs
git stash push -u -m "wip: per-tenant

172
00:12:49,128 --> 00:12:53,244
limits", fixes the incident,
commits, and runs git stash pop.

173
00:12:53,284 --> 00:12:56,855
Four commands, ten minutes.
That's what stash is for.

174
00:12:57,195 --> 00:13:00,600
The textbook's advice on the
message: always give -m.

175
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:05,670
Without it, entries are listed as WIP on
<branch> plus the subject of the base

176
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:08,074
commit, and five of those look alike.

177
00:13:08,414 --> 00:13:13,014
And when the interruption turns out to
last until tomorrow: for work that must

178
00:13:13,054 --> 00:13:16,703
outlive the day, a commit on a
branch is the better container.

179
00:13:16,743 --> 00:13:20,688
It shows in git log, can be pushed,
and no single command drops it.

180
00:13:20,728 --> 00:13:23,841
git stash branch converts
an entry into exactly that.

181
00:13:24,181 --> 00:13:28,537
For the engineer in the hook, the
lesson is the last of the five answers.

182
00:13:28,577 --> 00:13:33,295
They wanted a clean tree. git stash push
-u clears the same untracked files and

183
00:13:33,335 --> 00:13:37,932
keeps them as objects. That's the second
answer: what they should have typed.

184
00:13:39,772 --> 00:13:47,270
Your turn. Do Lab 8.6, "Stash and a pop
conflict", in lab-manual/m08-undo.md.

185
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:48,807
Predict before you type:

186
00:13:49,147 --> 00:13:54,759
After git stash push, what does git status
-s show, and where do refs/stash, HEAD

187
00:13:54,799 --> 00:13:56,294
and your branch point?

188
00:13:56,634 --> 00:14:01,389
When the pop conflicts: what are the two
marker labels, what is the exit status, and

189
00:14:01,429 --> 00:14:02,873
is the entry still listed?

190
00:14:03,213 --> 00:14:07,489
After you drop the entry in the failure
scenario: by which two routes can it be

191
00:14:07,529 --> 00:14:09,183
found, and what does each need?

192
00:14:09,523 --> 00:14:13,478
The challenge is Lab 8.5, "Clean
safely", in the same file.

193
00:14:13,518 --> 00:14:17,811
Write down the expected list of
every dry run before you run it.

194
00:14:19,651 --> 00:14:23,816
Q177: "What is inside a stash
entry and where is it stored?

195
00:14:23,856 --> 00:14:29,660
What happens when git stash pop conflicts?
Give three reasons not to use the stash

196
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:31,299
for long-term storage."

197
00:14:33,639 --> 00:14:38,513
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
describes an entry as objects and a ref, with

198
00:14:38,553 --> 00:14:41,964
the parents by role, and says
where older entries are kept.

199
00:14:42,004 --> 00:14:46,643
For the conflict it gives the state of
the working tree, the index and the stash

200
00:14:46,683 --> 00:14:51,440
list, and names both ways forward. The
three reasons should be consequences of the

201
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:55,710
storage you described in the first
part, not a separate list of opinions.

202
00:14:55,750 --> 00:14:59,687
If the reasons follow from the
mechanism, the answer holds together.

203
00:15:01,527 --> 00:15:04,454
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

204
00:15:04,794 --> 00:15:09,712
git clean deletes untracked files, which
were never objects, so nothing in Git can

205
00:15:09,752 --> 00:15:13,037
restore them. I run it with
-n first and read the list.

206
00:15:13,377 --> 00:15:18,908
A stash entry is a commit whose parents are
the old HEAD and the index, plus a third

207
00:15:18,948 --> 00:15:22,887
for untracked files with -u,
reachable only from refs/stash.

208
00:15:23,227 --> 00:15:27,898
pop is apply plus drop, and the drop
